 Interview Transcript: Medication Management and Technology Usage

Name: Dipendra Lal Basu
Age: 80 years old
Gender: Male


Interviewer: Okay, so we will take an interview today about your medicine and tech usage today. If you could start by saying your name and your age.

Dipendra Lal Basu: My name is Dipendra Lal Basu, I am 80 years old.

Interviewer: Please say something more about yourself. Do you take your medicines by yourself or does someone else help you with it?

Dipendra Lal Basu: I take my medicine by myself, nobody's helps is required. Sometimes doctors help is required if needed.

Interviewer: How do you take your medicines every day? And, how many medicines are prescribed to you?

Dipendra Lal Basu: Totally 8-10 medicines are prescribed to me. In mornings, I take 4-5, in noon I take 2, at night I take 4.

Interviewer: How do you keep track of which medicines you should take every day? How do you remember it or how do you keep track?

Dipendra Lal Basu: I have been following the written prescription for a very long time.

Interviewer: Have you ever used any tools to keep track of your medicines such as written notes/alarms/pillbox.

Dipendra Lal Basu: No, I only stick to the prescription. 

Interviewer: So, you mean you rely on your memory and the prescription copy.

Dipendra Lal Basu: Yes, always memory and the prescription copy.

Interviewer: Have you ever missed any dosages? Or do you miss them from time to time? If so, then why?

Dipendra Lal Basu: I miss only when the stock of my medicines run out or if they are unavailable to buy near me. 

Interviewer: You mean you only miss your dosages only when your medicines are unavailable?

Dipendra Lal Basu: Yes.

Interviewer: Meaning, you never miss due to your memory?

Dipendra Lal Basu: No, never.

Interviewer: Has it ever happened for you to manage your own medications, or maintaining your own routine?

Dipendra Lal Basu: Yes, sometimes when I am severely ill. Then I take help from doctors or healthcare professionals.

Interviewer:Do you use any technology in general? like mobile phones or computers or TV?

Dipendra Lal Basu: Yes, I use phones.

Interviewer: Do you think it is easier or is there even any need for you to use technology to maintain your medications?

Dipendra Lal Basu: No, I don't need those.

Interviewer: Do you ever find it hard to read or understand the instructions or the flow of the interfaces in any of the technologies that you use?

Dipendra Lal Basu:No, but if it happens I take help from my family and doctor.

Interviewer: What do you think would be helpful for you to use these techs more easily?

Dipendra Lal Basu: I use technology to ask the doctor to reduce the redundant medicines to make my life easier.

Interviewer: How do you contact with doctors or healthcare providers, and how frequently do you contact with them?

Dipendra Lal Basu: I contact over telephone, I go over to doctors physically every 2.5-3 months.

Interviewer:Do you have anything else to say regarding your experience with medicine and tech?

Dipendra Lal Basu: No, my only experience is I just follow the prescription and I do not go beyond it.

Interviewer: Any other suggestions for people of your age, if a tool is made which would remind you when to take your medicine, any suggestions on how to make these tools more helpful for them?

Dipendra Lal Basu: No, I have no idea about these.

Interviewer: Thank you.

Dipendra Lal Basu: Thank you.
